  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 AM EDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nUpper flow across area east of the Rockies is relatively weak as mid \nlevel ridging and a couple surface high pressure centers pair to \nkeep the area dry. As the first high pressure center moves into \nsoutheast Canada from the Northern Great Lakes, a backdoor cold \nfront sets up east of MI and north of OH, which may be able to \nadvect marine air into our east potentially setting them up for \nmorning fog Wed AM. Another northern stream wave and trailing \nsurface high pass by to the north of the area Wed through Thu time \nframe. Because the front washes out across the area Wed night, \nminimal lowering of temps occurs for Friday (from mid 60s to low 70s \nThu to low to upper 60s Fri). With the mid level ridging still \nstaunchly in place across the Plains States and Mississippi Valley, \ntroughing in the west struggles to advance eastward and surface lows \ntake a north-northeasterly track into Southcentral Canada. So, it \ncould be until the first part of the next work week before we \nget our next rain into the area. There are hints that the mid \nlevel ridge finally pushes eastward at this time and this helps \nsome combination of a diving wave within the northern stream and\nsome southern stream moisture to make it up this way to provide\nrain.",
